    "long_instructions": "Lesson 2, Simulating a Party, Level 1 of 3: generating one random value before the full party simulation. The real teaching guide's most common mistake here: forgetting to import random before calling randint, worth checking first if a student's stuck.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nYou are building a pizza party simulation to help you figure out how much pizza to order. This program simulates how much pizza a person will want by randomly picking either 1, 2, or 3 slices.\r\n\r\n**Do This**\r\n\r\n1. Import the `random` module into your program.\r\n2. Update the `get_random_slices` function to use the `random.randint` function to return a random number from 1 to 3 slices of pizza.\r\n3. Call the `get_random_slices` function and print the number of slices it generates.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nTo generate a random number, import the `random` module and then call `randint` with a minimum and maximum value. For example, to generate a random number from 1 to 10, you could write:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport random\r\n\r\nrandom.randint(1, 10)\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Reflection:** This level only generates one person's slice count. What do you expect students will find challenging once they have to do this for a whole party of people, not just one?\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n::: details [**See a solution**]\r\n```\r\nimport random\r\n\r\ndef get_random_slices():\r\n  return random.randint(1, 3)\r\n\r\nslices = get_random_slices()\r\nprint(slices)\r\n```\r\n:::",
